WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.300
An agent needs to total a customer’s
charges.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.900
It writes a short program: fetch the
charges,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.060
add them up, return the total. Submilli
gives that program a

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.020
place to run. The language, compiler,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.100
and runtime are designed together around
code an agent writes and

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.340
may need to repair. Let’s follow that
program and see what those choices

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.420
buy us.  Start with the language.
TypeScript gives the

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.780
agent familiar functions, loops, objects,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.860
and type annotations. Submilli implements
its own version so

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.900
it can change the rules where that helps
generated code.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.780
There is no any type to bypass checking.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.260
Raw JSON arrives as unknown. When the
program casts it to

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.420
a specific type, Submilli checks the
value’s shape at runtime.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.700
If an amount arrives as text instead of a
number,

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.100
the cast fails there. The agent gets a
failure at the bad assumption,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.020
before that value travels through the
calculation.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.180
Familiar syntax helps it write the
program;

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.580
stricter checks help it repair one.

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:10.233
The program uses Submilli’s standard
library and installed packages.

00:01:10.473 --> 00:01:14.633
Why not accept any N P M dependency? Those
libraries may

00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:17.513
expect Node’s filesystem, process model,

00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.273
or other facilities that this runtime does
not provide.

00:01:21.473 --> 00:01:25.193
Supporting them would mean carrying those
expectations into the execution

00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:27.993
environment. With Submilli packages,

00:01:28.073 --> 00:01:31.353
you choose the available integrations
before the run.

00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:35.513
Package authors put permission checks
around the actions they expose.

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:39.513
An ordinary N P M library does not supply
those checks.

00:01:39.593 --> 00:01:44.313
A billing package exposes a typed
operation such as listing charges.

00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:47.753
Dependencies are pinned and recorded in a
lockfile.

00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:51.673
The agent can compose the operations you
provided without assembling

00:01:51.753 --> 00:01:54.633
a new software environment for each task.

00:01:54.753 --> 00:01:58.873
That trades unrestricted library choice
for an interface the application

00:01:58.953 --> 00:02:03.113
can prepare and control.  The compiler
also helps the agent fix

00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:06.553
its work. Here, the program misspells
amount.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:10.793
The diagnostic marks the field, suggests
the correct spelling,

00:02:10.873 --> 00:02:14.953
and includes the type declaration. The
agent has a concrete correction

00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:18.153
to make. Once the program passes these
checks,

00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:20.553
Submilli compiles it to WebAssembly.

00:02:20.793 --> 00:02:24.793
Package modules are built ahead of the run
and linked alongside it.

00:02:24.873 --> 00:02:27.433
Both execute through the same runtime.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:31.913
This gives Submilli a defined instruction
format to execute and meter,

00:02:31.993 --> 00:02:35.033
while the agent continues to write
readable TypeScript.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.300
Your application sends the program to the
Submilli server over H T

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.460
T P or M C P. Inside that existing
process,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.700
the runtime creates an execution instance
and runs the compiled code

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.220
with Submilli’s WebAssembly interpreter.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.180
Each instance holds the program’s state
and has resource limits.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.620
The program can call supported host
operations,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.940
which handle work such as service
requests.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.180
This arrangement puts execution and its
controls together,

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.860
without starting a separate operating
system for each program.

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:12.733
That matters when you run many agents.

00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:16.773
A micro V M provides a hardware
virtualization boundary,

00:03:16.853 --> 00:03:19.733
with a guest operating system and memory
to provision.

00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:24.613
Submilli creates WebAssembly instances
inside the process it already

00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:28.613
has. Compilation and instance setup still
take work,

00:03:28.693 --> 00:03:32.133
but there is no guest operating system to
boot for each run.

00:03:32.373 --> 00:03:34.693
When one program waits for a service,

00:03:34.733 --> 00:03:37.973
the runtime can yield while other runs
make progress.

00:03:38.213 --> 00:03:41.573
It is a different isolation boundary from
a micro V M,

00:03:41.653 --> 00:03:45.853
so the runtime and its host operations
remain part of what you trust.

00:03:46.213 --> 00:03:48.973
Useful failures are another part of the
design.

00:03:49.253 --> 00:03:52.853
Submilli’s GitHub package can report that
a branch has moved since

00:03:52.893 --> 00:03:56.853
the agent read it. The agent can read the
current branch and reconsider

00:03:56.973 --> 00:04:01.013
its edit. A rate limit error can carry
retry timing when the service

00:04:01.093 --> 00:04:04.613
provides it. The caller can decide when to
try again;

00:04:04.693 --> 00:04:07.493
the package does not retry automatically.

00:04:07.653 --> 00:04:11.653
These errors distinguish a stale
assumption from a temporary limit.

00:04:11.773 --> 00:04:15.493
That gives the agent a specific next step
instead of repeating the

00:04:15.653 --> 00:04:19.813
same failed call. Purpose-built packages
can make the operation,

00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:23.733
its permission checks, and its recovery
information one documented

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:24.773
interface.

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:30.387
Now return to our billing example. We
build the package so that listing

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:34.547
charges first checks the requested
operation and customer.

00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:38.467
The runtime evaluates that check against
the blueprint you created

00:04:38.547 --> 00:04:41.347
and the context your application supplied.

00:04:41.427 --> 00:04:45.347
A rule can require the requested customer
to match Customer A.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:48.467
A request for Customer A can proceed.

00:04:48.547 --> 00:04:53.187
The same function called for Customer B
can be denied before the lookup.

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:56.867
The check sits inside the package, so it
is part of performing the

00:04:57.227 --> 00:05:01.267
operation. The generated program does not
get to decide whether its

00:05:01.347 --> 00:05:02.867
own request is allowed.

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:08.373
These choices work together. Familiar
TypeScript gives the agent

00:05:08.453 --> 00:05:10.773
a practical way to express the task.

00:05:11.253 --> 00:05:15.813
Stricter checks and specific errors help
it find and repair mistakes.

00:05:16.053 --> 00:05:20.133
WebAssembly lets the runtime execute and
meter the program inside

00:05:20.173 --> 00:05:23.973
an existing process. Packages provide the
operations,

00:05:24.053 --> 00:05:26.373
and blueprints define their permitted use.

00:05:26.773 --> 00:05:30.213
The result is an environment built for
generated code:

00:05:30.293 --> 00:05:34.133
the agent can compose useful work and
respond to failures,

00:05:34.213 --> 00:05:38.453
while your application defines the access
and resources it receives.
